data_len = count - hdr_size(file) - hdr_len;
Reason:
The variable `data_len` is declared as a signed `int`. When `count` (a `size_t` from user input) is less than the sum of the header sizes, the subtraction yields a negative value, which is stored in `data_len`. This negative value is then passed to `ib_create_send_mad()`, which expects an unsigned `size_t` parameter. The implicit conversion turns the negative value into a very large positive number, causing an out-of-bounds write in `alloc_send_rmpp_list()`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
size_t data_len;
if (check_sub_overflow(count, hdr_size(file) + hdr_len, &data_len)) {
    ret = -EINVAL;
    goto err_ah;
}
Reason:
Changing `data_len` to `size_t` (unsigned) and using `check_sub_overflow` to detect underflow ensures that if the subtraction would produce a negative result, the function returns `-EINVAL` early. This prevents the incorrect negative-to-unsigned conversion and the consequent out-of-bounds access in `ib_create_send_mad`.

